 "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/koko-eating-bananas/",
 "pattern": "Binary search on the answer",
 "statement": "Koko has `piles` of bananas and `h` hours. Each hour she picks one pile and eats up to `k` bananas from it (if the pile has fewer, she finishes it and waits). Return the **smallest** eating speed `k` that lets her finish every pile within `h` hours.",
 "example": {
  "input": "piles = [3,6,7,11], h = 8",
  "output": "4",
  "why": "At speed 4 the piles take 1 + 2 + 2 + 3 = 8 hours. At speed 3 they would take 10."
 },
 "more": [
  {
   "input": "piles = [30,11,23,4,20], h = 5",
   "output": "30"
  },
  {
   "input": "piles = [30,11,23,4,20], h = 6",
   "output": "23"
  },
  {
   "input": "piles = [1], h = 1",
   "output": "1"
  }
 ],
 "idea": [
  "We are not searching the array — we are searching for a **number**: the speed `k`, somewhere between 1 and the largest pile.",
  "Speeds have a clear order: if speed `k` is fast enough, every faster speed is too; if it is too slow, every slower speed is too. That *no, no, no, yes, yes, yes* pattern is what binary search needs.",
  "Test the middle speed by adding up ⌈pile / k⌉ hours for every pile. Too slow → search the faster half. Fast enough → remember it and search the slower half."
 ],
 "code": "class Solution:\n    def minEatingSpeed(self, piles: List[int], h: int) -> int:\n        left, right = 1, max(piles)\n\n        while left < right:\n            mid = left + (right - left) // 2\n            hours = sum((pile + mid - 1) // mid for pile in piles)\n\n            if hours > h:\n                left = mid + 1\n            else:\n                right = mid\n\n        return left",
 "complexity": {
  "time": "O(n · log m)",
  "timeWhy": "log m speeds are tested (m = largest pile), and each test adds up all n piles.",
  "space": "O(1)",
  "spaceWhy": "Only a few numbers are stored."
 },
